# Vendored shared-process-kit artifacts are hashed byte-for-byte by the
# tier-1 drift test (tests/test_kit_drift.py), which splits each file at its
# END KIT PROVENANCE marker and asserts the body sha256 equals the kit
# manifest value. A checkout that rewrote CRLF<->LF would change the body
# bytes and break the hash, so these paths are pinned to LF and marked
# non-text. snap.sh sets core.autocrlf false operationally for the same
# reason; this rule is the in-repo half.
#
# The hooks are pinned by GLOB rather than one line per file, since 2026-08-11.
# The enumerated form was a list that had to be remembered: the 0.2.18 gate
# arrived with `ci_state.py` and `ci_state_mutations.py` beside it, both
# hash-pinned by the same drift test, and neither would have been covered until
# someone thought to add two lines here. `.claude/tools/**` was already a glob
# for that reason. Pinning the one repository-owned hook too is deliberate and
# harmless: it is checked in as LF already.
.claude/hooks/** text eol=lf
.claude/agents/incident-analyst.md text eol=lf
.claude/tools/** text eol=lf
# The DEPLOYED half of the OQ-56 two-file shape. Both deployed copies are
# byte-compared against their stamped of-record by the drift test, so a
# checkout that rewrote line endings would fail the comparison rather than the
# hash, which is the same defect wearing a different message. The agents line
# above is the first of these two; this is the second, added 2026-08-11 with
# the version-control skill.
.claude/skills/version-control/SKILL.md text eol=lf

# The goldens are byte-exact fixtures: their bytes ARE the assertion.
# They are read with read_text, whose universal-newline translation
# hides a line-ending rewrite from the comparison, so a CRLF checkout
# passes for a reason unrelated to the emitter being right. Pinning
# them to LF makes the file on disk the same on every machine, which
# is what "byte-exact" has to mean before the phrase is worth writing.
# The whitespace pre-commit hooks are excluded from this directory for
# the same reason (.pre-commit-config.yaml), after one of them rewrote
# a golden's last byte on 2026-08-07.
tests/goldens/** text eol=lf

# The fixtures are the same argument as the goldens above, and they cost
# two tier-1 failures on 2026-08-19 before anyone made it. A fixture read
# as BYTES carries its line endings into the assertion, so an unpinned one
# is CRLF on Windows and LF on Linux and the case measures the checkout.
# Two cases did: one asserted the fixture was CRLF and fired its own
# non-vacuity guard on every Linux runner, the other split on a CRLF that
# was not there. Pinning them to LF makes the file on disk the same
# everywhere, and the guard beside it (tests/test_matrix.py) is what
# refuses a CR arriving in one.
#
# `-text` RATHER THAN `eol=lf`, and the difference is the fixtures' whole
# purpose. Several of these are captured solver output, and their CRLF is
# what the solver wrote: normalizing them would make the committed bytes
# disagree with the run they record. `-text` tells git to convert
# nothing in either direction, so the file on disk is the file in the
# index on every platform, which is the property a byte-reading case
# needs and the only one it needs.
tests/fixtures/** -text
